$$ I_n = \int_1^0 (1-(1-u))^n \frac{-1}{2\sqrt{1-u}} du = \int_0^1 u^n \frac{1}{2\sqrt{1-u}} du = \frac{1}{2} \int_0^1 \frac{u^n}{\sqrt{1-u}} du $$
As $n \to \infty$, $u^n \to 0$ for $0 < u < 1$. We are integrating $u^n$ from 0 to 1. Since $u^n$ is continuous on $[0,1]$, we can apply the Dominated Convergence Theorem.

We can estimate the integral by comparing it to the integral of $u^n$ from 0 to 1, which is $\int_0^1 u^n du = \frac{1}{n+1}$.
Thus $I_n \approx \frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{n+1}$.
Then
$$ L = \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} \sqrt{n} \int_0^1 (1-x^2)^n dx \approx \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} \sqrt{n} \frac{1}{2(n+1)} = \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}} \frac{\sqrt{n}}{n+1} = \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}} \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}+1/\sqrt{n}} = 0 $$

